To call a .NET Core API from a React app that is secured with Azure AD B2C, follow these steps:


---

Step 1: Set Up Azure AD B2C

Before integrating authentication in your React app and API, ensure you have Azure AD B2C configured.

1. Create an Azure AD B2C Tenant (if not already created).


2. Register Two Applications in Azure AD B2C:

React App (Frontend)

.NET Core API (Backend)




Step 1.1: Register the React App

1. Go to Azure Portal → Search for Azure AD B2C.


2. App registrations → New registration.


3. Enter a name (e.g., ReactApp).


4. Select supported account types → "Accounts in any identity provider or organizational directory (for authentication using user flows)".


5. Redirect URI: Choose Single-page application (SPA) and enter your React app URL (e.g., http://localhost:3000).


6. Click Register.


7. Under "Authentication", enable Access tokens & ID tokens.


8. Under "API permissions", add API permissions for your backend API.


9. Under "Certificates & secrets", generate a client secret and copy it.




---

Step 1.2: Register the .NET Core API

1. App registrations → New registration.


2. Enter a name (e.g., MyBackendAPI).


3. Select Supported account types → Same as above.


4. Expose API:

Click "Expose an API" → Add a Scope (e.g., user_impersonation).

Set scope name, description, and select Admin consent required → Save.



5. Under "API permissions", add Microsoft Graph - OpenID & offline_access.


6. Save Application (client) ID and Directory (tenant) ID.




---

Step 2: Implement Authentication in React App

Now, configure authentication in your React App using MSAL.js (Microsoft Authentication Library).

Step 2.1: Install Required Packages

npm install @azure/msal-browser @azure/msal-react axios

Step 2.2: Configure MSAL in React

Create a new file authConfig.js in your React app:

import { PublicClientApplication } from "@azure/msal-browser";

const msalConfig = {
  auth: {
    clientId: "YOUR_REACT_APP_CLIENT_ID",  // Replace with React app Client ID
    authority: "https://YOUR_TENANT_NAME.b2clogin.com/YOUR_TENANT_ID/B2C_1A_SIGNUP_SIGNIN", // Replace with policy
    redirectUri: "http://localhost:3000",  
  },
};

export const msalInstance = new PublicClientApplication(msalConfig);

Step 2.3: Set Up Authentication Context

Modify index.js to wrap your app with MSAL Provider:

import React from "react";
import ReactDOM from "react-dom";
import { MsalProvider } from "@azure/msal-react";
import App from "./App";
import { msalInstance } from "./authConfig";

ReactDOM.render(
  <MsalProvider instance={msalInstance}>
    <App />
  </MsalProvider>,
  document.getElementById("root")
);

Step 2.4: Implement Login in React Component

In App.js or Login.js:

import React from "react";
import { useMsal } from "@azure/msal-react";

const LoginButton = () => {
  const { instance } = useMsal();

  const login = async () => {
    try {
      await instance.loginPopup({
        scopes: ["https://YOUR_TENANT_NAME.onmicrosoft.com/YOUR_API_SCOPE/user_impersonation"],
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  };

  return <button onClick={login}>Login with Azure B2C</button>;
};

export default LoginButton;


---

Step 3: Protect .NET Core API with JWT Authentication

Step 3.1: Install Required NuGet Packages

Run the following command:

dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer

Step 3.2: Configure Authentication in appsettings.json

Add this section:

"AzureAdB2C": {
  "Instance": "https://YOUR_TENANT_NAME.b2clogin.com/",
  "ClientId": "YOUR_API_CLIENT_ID",
  "Domain": "YOUR_TENANT_NAME.onmicrosoft.com",
  "SignUpSignInPolicyId": "B2C_1A_SIGNUP_SIGNIN"
}

Step 3.3: Configure JWT Authentication in Program.cs

Modify Program.cs to add authentication:

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;
using Microsoft.Identity.Web;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration.GetSection("AzureAdB2C"));

builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
builder.Services.AddControllers();

var app = builder.Build();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();


